title The jail's onboarding, and one seat table written six times
prompt Debug the ai-jail display fault blamed on NixOS; unify the week's launch scripts and batons; plan the Earth-ship vocabulary consolidation and the molt breach of the aether dual categorizations; name blind spots; baton new prin recur.
think Opened the lap with the cold roster and read the card whole. Line one printed staged_uncommitted=0, stashed_entries=0, tree_at_open=a5328d506b09, roster_receipt=none, and 28 guards went green with zero red before I stopped the pass.
think Stopping it was a judgment call named here rather than hidden: sow_witness alone ran past four minutes, a full cold pass on this clone measures in hours by %360 and %374's own numbers, and the hot pass after git add is the one the card says measures the tree the commit ships. Paying both serially would have cost a multiple of the work. The cold open's line-one readings were captured first; the full hot pass runs at close.
think The display fault had two candidate causes and I measured both rather than taking either. Inside the jail against outside on metal: ai-jail drops TERMINFO_DIRS and LOCALE_ARCHIVE from the environment, which reads exactly like the cause -- and both are harmless, infocmp tmux-256color resolving through ncurses' compiled-in path and a two-character multibyte string reading 2 from wc -m inside and out. TERM and COLORTERM both survive, so the color depth is whole.
think What did not survive was one file. Inside the jail, [ -f ~/.claude.json ] answers ABSENT; on the host it is 42 KB carrying hasCompletedOnboarding true. Claude Code keeps that flag and the chosen theme in a FILE beside ~/.claude/, and --private-home makes HOME a tmpfs, so the mount that DID land delivered settings.json's theme and not the record that the question had been asked.
think The mount existed and was conditional on loops/claude/dot-claude.json already existing -- and the only process that writes it is the jailed Claude, into the tmpfs the exit discards. The condition could never come true on a fresh pier, and an absent bind source is skipped rather than refused, so the failing direction was silent.
obs The NixOS configuration is not at fault and no rebuild was needed. That is the answer to the question actually asked, and it was reached by measuring the two plausible explanations rather than by rebuilding and seeing.
obs The sharper half is where the fault had already been found. tools/e/enclosure.conf.example says "Optional: copy host ~/.claude.json ... for quieter Claude starts" and the nixos-guide tabulates the same mount as "(optional seed)". Both are accurate descriptions of a workaround, written by a hand that hit this and stepped around it. A workaround written into the documentation is a red somebody already found.
obs The three sibling mounts in the same block take the opposite shape and are correct -- CLAUDE_STATE, CURSOR_AGENT_STATE and CODEX_STATE are each mkdir -p'd then mounted unconditionally, and the codex one carries a comment arguing exactly why. The rule was written, argued, and applied to three of the four things it governs.
obs Seeded with empty JSON rather than a hand-written hasCompletedOnboarding key. Guessing another program's config schema is a claim no witness in this tree can hold; Claude writes its own record on the first jailed run and it persists.
obs Pond's record already declared this mount at pond/enclosure_policy.kyri line 133, so pond_policy_launcher's enforced unspelled_maps reading is untouched -- the launcher had been under-building what the policy described, which is the reported direction.
obs agent_jail_witness.sh was RED at its first leg on this pier and unrostered, so nothing said so. Three stale pins in one guard: it grepped the dry-run plan for .claude-state and .cursor-agent-state, the directories the 20260827 one-room adoption moved to loops/; it sourced tools/e/enclosure.conf under set -u on a pier that has none; and it smoke-tested cursor-agent unconditionally. REDS %360's unheard-guard class exactly.
obs Killing the cold roster mid-pass stranded 2,178 .tpr files in the tracked tree -- tools/fixtures/t/tool_path_repoint_scan.sh writes its temporary beside each file it visits and removes it in a per-file loop, so an interrupt leaves one per file already seen. Cleaned to zero untracked before any edit. Named as a recommendation rather than repaired: it is out of this lap's lane and the fix is a trap-cleaned scratch directory.
obs The fleet measured rather than remembered: fleet_rearm walks nine seats and finds three, all Earth. The six aether seats are absent directories. The seat-to-tree-to-engine binding is spelled in six independent tables across two executables, and the two files already disagree -- fleet-loop.sh admits six seat names, fleet_rearm.sh reports nine, and the furrow/harvest remap exists in one and not the other.
obs A drift in the captain's own baton: expanding-prompts/20260904-171306 names door two as the pier's ~/grain clone, the card says no ~/grain on Dallas, and the filesystem agrees with the card. The elder prompt is dated testimony and keeps its words; the new baton carries the correction.
obs The card had 25 bytes of headroom against its 24,576 bound, so every addition had to be a swap. Condensing three now-shelved rows from summary to pointer paid for the %408 lines with room to spare -- 24,525 at close.
loom roster=standing_equipment cold_guards_green=28 cold_red=0 stopped_by_hand=yes sow_witness_wall_s=245
